# A small JEPA world model: the encoder knows, the predictor does not add

*Ravindu Somawansa — draft, 2026-11-01. Every number below is generated by `paper/make_paper.py` from `repro/*/expected/`; its source is in `paper/numbers.json`.*

## Abstract

We trained a LeWM-style JEPA world model (W1) on {{teleop_eps}} teleoperated demonstrations of one SO-101 tabletop task for ${{cost_w1}} and ran it through {{rows}} pre-registered uses taken from published world-model papers. The encoder reads the outcome: on {{n}} policy rollouts it never saw in training, a linear probe on the final-frame latent separates success from failure at AUROC {{rest_W1}}, level with a hand-built colour judge ({{rest_judge}}) and above a frozen DINOv2-S, the commanded joints and policy identity. The predictor adds nothing: at the grasp anchor the imagined latent reads the grasp worse than the current one ({{c1_imagined}} vs {{c1_current}}), and imagining forward does not add to what the current latent already reads. Every use that needs a multi-step rollout (ranking, surprise monitoring, risk, multi-scale planning) misses its bar. One rig, one model, one data scale; the controls are exploratory.

## 1. Introduction

World-model papers report the use that worked. We report every use we pre-registered for one small JEPA world model on one ${{arm_cost}} arm: {{rows}} gates, each with a bar, a dataset and a verdict, logged in a public register ({{bar_first}} bars committed before their result, {{same_commit}} in the same commit as the result, and {{no_class}} row, the on-device latency of the pretrained RoboJEPA / V-JEPA 2.1, that is not a W1 use and has no pre-registration class). Of the {{rows}}, {{n_pass}} pass, {{n_met}} is met as predicted, {{n_np}} are not proven and {{n_fail}} fail or are no-go (the RoboJEPA row is one of the {{n_fail}}).

The result splits along the model. The *encoder* reads the episode's outcome from the current frame, better than a frozen foundation encoder or the robot's own joint commands, including at the grasp anchor, {{anc_lead}} frames before the gripper first closes. The *predictor*, which every forecasting use relies on, does not improve on that by imagining forward. Contribution: (i) the register, with a load audit of every checkpoint use (Fig. 1); (ii) controls that separate encoder from predictor (Table 2, Fig. 2); (iii) the reading that, at this data scale, the predictor is the weak link.

**Caveats stated up front.** The encoder readouts are linear probes fit with leave-one-session-out (LOSO) cross-validation on {{n}} rollouts ({{success}} success / {{failure}} failure, {{sessions}} sessions) with operator labels; W1 was trained on teleop only and never saw them, but the controls were chosen after the data and are **exploratory**. Two of the three passes have same-commit bars.

## 2. Setup

**Robot and data.** One SO-101 arm, two cameras (top, wrist), one task family (put a bowl in another). W1 is trained on `ravediamond/so101_bowls_all_trimmed` ({{train_eps}} train / {{val_eps}} validation episodes, split by grid layout); the evaluation rollouts are `ravediamond/so101-bowls-policy-rollouts`, a separate dataset of SmolVLA rollouts recorded after W1 was trained.

**Model.** W1 follows the LeWM recipe (multi-view ViT encoder, action-conditioned predictor, SIGReg), trained on one rented GPU. **Controls.** W2′: frozen DINOv2-S, mean-pooled, top + wrist. Joints: the commanded joint vector. G9c-3: a hand-built colour judge on the final frame (no learning). Policy identity: which checkpoint ran the episode.

**Load audit.** One early gate (G11-A) loaded W1 with `strict=False` and ran a random encoder. We therefore re-traced the load path of every use: {{audit_trained}} trained, {{audit_random}} random (G11-A, already voided), 0 unknown. The VLA-JEPA world-model block loads {{wm_keys}} / {{wm_keys}} keys ({{wm_missing}} missing).

## 3. The register

Figure 1 lists the {{rows}} uses with what each takes from W1: the encoder alone ({{uses_encoder}}), the predictor ({{uses_predictor}}) or both ({{uses_both}}).

![The register: every pre-registered use of W1 (and the W2 / RoboJEPA rows beside it), what it takes from the model, whether its bar was committed before the result, and the verdict. Source: repro/register/expected/FEASIBILITY.md §2 and uses.json.](fig/register.pdf){wide}

*Encoder reads.* G10 step 1 reads the outcome at rest (pass, retrospective, same-commit); W1-C1's current-latent arm reads the grasp at the anchor ({{c1_current}}). The pose probes (LeJEPA) measure what else the latent holds: the encoder holds about half of the joint variance linearly (R² {{lejepa_w1}}; the frozen DINOv2-S {{lejepa_w2p}}). Encoder uses that read progress or stage rather than outcome fail: G6a, G6b, W1-B, and SURVEYOR's latent-distance scale (τ {{surv_tau_W1}}, paper {{surv_paper}}).

*Predictor forecasts.* G0's open-loop prediction beats copy-last ({{g0_err}} vs {{g0_copy}} at K = 30): the predictor predicts its own latent. Every use of that prediction fails: G1b ranks chunks at top-1 {{g1b_top1}} (bar {{g1b_bar}}) while the commanded actions alone reach {{g1b_actions}}; W1-A's surprise catches {{w1a_hits}}; W1-C1's imagined latent ({{c1_imagined}}) is below the current one; HWM finds no crossover; W1-C2, W1-R, REAP and G11-B-cal all miss their bars.

## 4. What the encoder knows

Table 2: Outcome AUROC on the same {{n}} rollouts. Exploratory (controls chosen after the data), LOSO linear probes, operator labels. At rest: 95 % episode-bootstrap CI; at the anchor: mean of 5 seeds.

| feature | at rest | grasp anchor ({{anc_scored}} scored, {{anc_fail}} fail) |
|---|---|---|
| W1 (ours) | {{rest_W1}} [{{rest_W1_lo}}, {{rest_W1_hi}}] | {{anc_W1}} |
| colour judge G9c-3 (no learning) | {{rest_judge}} [{{rest_judge_lo}}, {{rest_judge_hi}}] | — |
| W2′ frozen DINOv2-S | {{rest_W2p}} [{{rest_W2p_lo}}, {{rest_W2p_hi}}] | {{anc_W2p}} |
| commanded joints | {{rest_joints}} [{{rest_joints_lo}}, {{rest_joints_hi}}] | {{anc_joints}} |
| policy identity | {{rest_policy}} [{{rest_policy_lo}}, {{rest_policy_hi}}] | — |

![Table 2 as a dot plot. Left: AUROC at rest with its 95 % bootstrap CI. Right: AUROC at the grasp anchor, mean of 5 seeds with the seed range (not a CI). Exploratory, LOSO, operator labels.](fig/table2.pdf)

At rest W1 is level with the colour judge (CIs overlap) and above the frozen encoder and the joints; at the grasp anchor, where no judge applies, it leads both controls. The pose readers hold the joints better (§3) but the outcome worse: the encoder holds outcome information they do not. The original G10 gate scored W1 at {{g10_rest}}, but against the G9c judge's labels, not the operator's, on {{g10_n}} rollouts; the rerun here, on the {{n}} Orin rollouts against operator labels with the controls beside it, gives {{rest_W1}}.

## 5. Where the predictor fails

The cleanest contrast is W1-C1: same encoder, same anchor, current latent {{c1_current}} vs imagined {{c1_imagined}} (bar {{c1_bar}} over current). Imagining forward loses outcome information the encoder had. On SURVEYOR's scale check, W2′ (τ {{surv_tau_W2p}}) and the joints (τ {{surv_tau_joints}}) pass where W1 fails, which points at W1's latent geometry rather than the task.

*Exploratory, two further lines.* (1) On the distillation probe's §30 term (pooled, {{g5_val}} teleop validation episodes), trained W1 ({{g5_w1}}) is not distinguishable from its own random init ({{g5_rand}}): difference {{g5_diff}}, 95 % paired episode-bootstrap CI [{{g5_lo}}, {{g5_hi}}] (`gap5_pooled_rerun.json`). The term is sensitive to BLAS / ridge conditioning: the same untrained network scored {{g5_unstable}} on one machine and {{g5_rand}} / {{g5_orin}} on two others. (2) The surprise scorer that gives W1 a class-5 AUROC of {{m1_auroc}} (below its sanity bar) reproduces G0's K = 30 corruption ratios (cross-episode {{m1_xep}} vs {{m1_xep_g0}}, constant-first {{m1_cf}} vs {{m1_cf_g0}}; `M1_diag_g0_repro.json`), so {{m1_auroc}} is a real value, not a load or scorer bug.

*Objection: G10 step 2 is a predictive pass.* The imagined latent beat policy identity by {{g10_anticip}} — but that bar is same-commit and retrospective, and the two bar-first reruns of the same idea (W1-C1, G11-B-cal) fail.

## 6. Related work

LeWorldModel (2603.19312) trains the JEPA recipe we use end to end from pixels and plans in Push-T and simulation; its planning use fails here (G1b, HWM). LeJEPA identifiability (2605.26379) predicts SIGReg latents identify state up to anisotropy, which holds here partially. DINO-WM (2411.04983) plans on frozen DINOv2 patches, our W2 / W2′ design. V-JEPA 2.1 (2603.14482) is too slow on our Orin. ContactGuard (2608.13438) finds imagined-latent probes beat current-latent ones on grasp tasks; we find the opposite (W1-C1). ReAP (2610.16309) predicts action-conditioned risk; its re-test misses its bar here. Two results agree with ours from the control side: SaiVLA-JEPA (2610.38508) finds a training-only future-latent head adds {{sai_gain}} points to control on {{sai_eps}} paired episodes, and HIJP (2610.23615) finds LeWM below goal-conditioned behaviour cloning in simulation.

## 7. Limitations

Exploratory controls: {{n}} rollouts, operator labels, probes chosen after the data; fresh rollouts scored under a bar committed first are the test. One rig, one model, one data scale: a second scale point ({{scale_eps}} episodes) is costed and queued separately, and the scale curve belongs to the longer version. Not run: W1-A surprise and W1-C1 imagined with W2′ / joints (each needs its own predictor), G6a T1 with the controls (needs a reimplementation). G0 and G10 bars are same-commit.

## 8. Conclusion

On a small teleop dataset, a JEPA world model learns an encoder that reads how the episode went, a short horizon ahead included, and a predictor whose imagination adds nothing to it. Reporting every pre-registered use, not the best one, is what makes that visible.
